 1. print("a") writes                                    'a\n'
 2. print("a\n") adds its newline anyway                 'a\n\n'
 3. print(1, 2) joins with sep=' ' on one line           '1 2\n'
 4. print([1, [2, 3]]) writes the list's repr            '[1, [2, 3]]\n'
 5. print(None) writes the word None                     'None\n'
 6. print([]) writes []                                  '[]\n'
 7. print() with no argument                             '\n'
 8. print("a", "b", sep="", end="") is Ruby's print      'ab'
 9. print(None, 1.0, end='') uses str, None included     'None 1.0'
10. print(repr("s")) is the p spelling                   "'s'\n"
11. print(repr(1), repr(2)) stays on one line            '1 2\n'
12.   print returns                                      None
13.   there is no p; repr(x) returns the string          "'s'"
14.   print returns                                      None
15. print(obj) uses str; print(repr(obj)) uses repr      'Foo-str\n'; 'Foo-repr\n'
16. f"{obj}" uses str; print([obj]) uses repr of each    'Foo-str\n'; '[Foo-repr]\n'
17. print([obj]) uses repr of each element               '[Foo-repr]\n'
18. print(h) writes one line, this long                  187
19. pprint(h, sort_dicts=False) wraps at 80, in order:   
{'name': 'Ada Lovelace',
 'languages': ['ruby', 'python', 'perl'],
 'address': {'city': 'London', 'country': 'United Kingdom'},
 'notes': 'a fairly long string to push past eighty columns'}
20.   pprint(h) default sort_dicts=True: first line      {'address': {'city': 'London', 'country': 'United Kingdom'},
21.   pprint returns                                     None
22. sys.stdout.write('caf\N{...}\n') returns characters  5
23. sys.stdout.write takes one string, not several       5
24. print("%05.1f|%s" % (3.14159, "x")) is format + write '003.1|x\n'
25. no << on streams: write returns an int, no chain     n/a
26. print(file=sys.stderr) is the spelling of both       'to stderr\nwarned twice\n'
27.   several arguments stay on one line, like print     2
